  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Black Matrilineage, Photography, and Representation shows early but meaningful reach for a 2022 scholarly book: it has been downloaded 2,437 times, is available in open access form, and has already been used in a recorded book talk. Its strongest signals point to sustained interest in questions of Black representation, gender equality, and the cultural politics of photography.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Lesly Deschler Canossi and Zoraida Lopez-Diago\u2019s Black Matrilineage, Photography, and Representation is a scholarly book published by Leuven University Press that engages Black maternal experience, visual culture, and the politics of representation. The record shows a clear pattern of use rather than citation-based uptake: the book has been downloaded 2,437 times through OAPEN, indicating steady reader engagement with the work as an accessible scholarly resource.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is also associated with open access availability in a hybrid model, which likely supports its reach across research, teaching, and public-interest reading. A recorded book talk on YouTube provides evidence of teaching or discussion use, suggesting the work has already entered broader interpretive conversations beyond the publisher\u2019s page. Together, these signals point to a book that is being encountered and discussed, even though formal citation accumulation has not yet developed in the indexed record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Its topical alignment is especially strong around gender equality, consistent with the volume\u2019s focus on Black female and maternal embodiment, health, sexuality, and photography. The metadata also indicates support from KU Leuven and Knowledge Unlatched, which helps situate the book within funded open scholarship and may have contributed to its accessibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the impact profile is best described as early-stage but substantive. The evidence emphasizes readership, openness, and thematic relevance: the book is being accessed, discussed, and positioned within debates on representation and gender, while the citation record remains quiet at present.</p>",
